## ABSTRACT
Composites of MOFs and aerogels (MOFACs), as a new class of nanostructured materials, attract increasing attention due to their favorable adsorption properties. Herein, UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ -CS aerogel monolith (UNCAM) was synthesized by covalent crosslinking with hierarchical structure, exhibiting effective and stable adsorption of Pb(II) ions. The aerogel monolith containing $50\%$ MOF particles possesses a ultra-low density of $15.8~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which is mainly attributed to the highly porous structure. Meantime, UNCAM can be described more suitable by the pseudo-second-order model and shows a higher mass transfer rate. The highest $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption capacity of aerogel monolith is up to $102.03\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ $(1.612{\times}10^{6}\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3})$ , which is comparable to literature reports. The adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ by UNCAM is an endothermic and spontaneous processand consistent with the Langmuir model, indicating that the adsorption process belongs to monolayer adsorption and chemisorption.  Furthermore, the adsorption mechanisms of coordination interaction between N and Pb (II) were confirmed, and the
O also played a synergistic role in adsorption on a certain degree. Lastly, The UNCAM retained $90.12\%$ adsorption ability after three cycles. This strategy may provide an effective and versatile pathway to convert the bulk MOF particles into a shapeable form and sequentially branch out their applications in pollutant treatment fields.
Keywords: UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , Aerogel, Covalent crosslinking, Adsorption, Heavy metal 1. Introduction
"Heavy metals" devote a metal element with a density of over $5~\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which are seriously harmful to human health and environment, mainly refers to lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic, chromium and copper, et. al [1]. Among them, Pb(II) can accumulate in the food chain system, seriously damaging the human central nervous system, immune system and skeletal development. Therefore, Pb(II) ions are considered to be one of the most toxic metal ions and have been documented as an importantly preferential issue by the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) [2]. The typical methods to remove Pb(II) include flocculation precipitation, membrane filtration, adsorption, ion exchange and so on [3-5]. In these methods, adsorption is the most common technique with the merits of easy operation and low cost. However, traditional adsorbents often exhibit poor adsorption capacities and adverse flexibility such as activated carbons, which motivate us to develop innovate and effective adsorbents [5]. MOFs, the abbreviation of metal-organic framework（s）, are a class of widely used metal-organic porous and crystalline materials possessing ultra-high surface area. In recent years, MOFs have aroused more and more attentions for absorbing toxic heavy metals due to their distinct porosity and adsorption capacity [6-17]. Particularly, ZrMOFs are notoriously used to remove heavy metal ions, radionuclides, organic pollutants and other inorganic substances via adsorption, which is due to the unprecedented thermal and chemical stability [18-23]. However, the natural form of Zr
MOFs is microcrystalline powders. Though this state is suitable for research purposes, the disadvantages of Zr-MOFs, such as strong pressure drops in packed beds, mass transfer limitations, dust formation, and poor handling, greatly limit their industrial applicability [24].
Heretofore, there are usually two ways to prepare MOF monoliths. Firstly, MOFs are grown or deposited on special supported materials, for instance ceramic [25] or polymeric monolith [26], fibers [27], foams [28-30] and aerogels [31-42]. However, low loading of MOF particles on the surface of the substrate limits their applications [43]. The second method involves mechanical compression and granulates the pure MOF into powders. Meantime, proper granulation pressure should be carefully considered because this approach can lead to pressure-induced losing of both microporosity and crystallinity in some cases. [44,45] Therefore, a new strategy to prepare novel stable MOF monoliths with highly mass transfer capacity, porosity and adsorption capacity is highly desired.
Chitosan (CS) is a widely known chitin deacetylated derivative with free $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and - OH functional groups which can adsorp heavy metals [46]. In most cases, chitosan has a tendency to dissolve or agglomerate in waste water, thus the adsorption capacity is inefficient [25, 47]. The combination of chitosan and rigid materials for adsorption is an effective way to solve the above problems [47]. Based on aforementioned, herein, we present a new strategy to design and preparation of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ -CS aerogel monolith (UNCAM) adsorbent, which can not only graft MOF particles into monolithic with high mass transfer and capacity, but also maintain the adsorption capacity of chitosan. The strategy use UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (a famous kind of Zr-MOF) particles and chitosan as the building blocks with glutaraldehyde as the cross-linking agent by covalent crosslinking, which is totally different from the usually MOF particles embedded or deposition on the surface of aerogel. Such aerogel monoliths have hierarchical porous structure with interconnected macroporous and microporous structures which can provide excellent flow-through properties and permeability, significantly diminish the resistance of mass transfer. The density and the amount of adsorption for $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ of UNCAM are $15.8~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $104~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ , respectively. Meantime, the mass of MOF particles attain $50\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ in UNCAM. In addition, the adsorption behaviors of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ were also researched on UNCAM and UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , such as adsorption isotherm models, kinetics and thermodynamic parameters. The adsorption mechanisms of coordination interaction between N and Pb (II) were confirmed. And the O also played a synergistic a certain role in adsorption. Finally, the recyclability of UNCAM was also investigated. The adsorption capacity of UNCAM has not been significantly reduced after 3 cycles [25, 47].
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
All reagents were obtained from commercial sources and without further purification. CS (number average molecular weight is 500000), 2-aminoterephthalic acid (BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ ), zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4,})}$ , N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) glutaraldehyde (50 $w t\%$ ) and Formic acid $(88\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin.
## 2.2. Preparation of the MOF
UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ was prepared following a reported reference [25] with minimal modifications. In a typical procedure, $0.249\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC and $0.36~\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ were totally dissolved in $54~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and $6\mathrm{mL}$ of formic acid. Then, the mixed solution was poured into a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and maintained at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the resulting solid powders were collected by centrifugation, washed three times using methanol and dichloromethane, and vacuum-dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of UNCAM
A $0.8\%$ (w/v) CS solution was obtained by dissolving CS $\left(0.08\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ in $(1\%\ \mathrm{v/v)}$ 100 mL of formic acid. UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ powders were added to $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of $0.8\%$ (w/v) CS solution $\mathrm{(UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ : CS $(0.8\%)=1:1$ (mass ratio)) to disperse into an emulsion. Then $15~\mathrm{\mL}$ of glutaraldehyde was added into this emulsion to participate the crosslinking reaction, and the emulsion was kept at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ . The UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ -CS hydrogel monolith (UNCHM) prepared by the aforementioned process were immersed in $(1\%\ \mathrm{v/v)}$ aqueous formic acid and $0.1\mathrm{mol}$ sodium carbonate for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . UNCHM was frozen using liquid nitrogen and vacuum freeze-dried for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which were then transformed into UNCAM after washing with deionized water.
## 2.4. Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra were measured ranging from $5\mathrm{-}50^{\circ}$ on a Siemens D5005 diffractometer. The IR spectrum was tested on an Alpha Centaurt FT/IR spectrophotometer. The $\Nu_{2}$ sorption measurement was implemented on an automatic volumetric adsorption equipment (BELSORP-mini II). The morphology of the materials was observed using scanning electron microscopy (SEM, S-4800, Hitachi, Japan). A thermogravimetric (TG) analysis of the samples was manipulated using a Perkin–Elmer TG7 analyzer heated from room temperature to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen at the heating rate of $10\mathrm{^{\circ}C\cdot m i n^{-1}}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses were performed on a VG ESCALABMKII spectrometer with an Al-Kα (1486.6 eV) achromatic X-ray source. The Pb(II) concentration was tested using Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS, Thermo Scientific-X).
## 2.5. Batch adsorption experiments
The adsorption experiment was conducted as follows: firstly, $5\mathrm{mL}$ solutions of Pb(II) 1 $(100\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , adjust $\mathrm{pH}{=}5$ with ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ ) was added to a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ glass bottle, and $2\:\mathrm{mg}$ of
UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ powders were added into the glass bottle. Then, the adsorption test was initiated in the water bath shaker at $20{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In the end, the mixture solution was treated with the centrifugal treatment. The adsorption amount of CS was measured under the same conditions. The UNCAM was extracted with tweezers without centrifugation after the adsorption.
The equation to calculate the mass of metal adsorbed $\mathrm{(mg)}$ versus per unit mass of the adsorbent q was listed as the following (Eq. (1)):
$$
q=V(c_{0}-c_{e})/m
$$
where $\mathrm{v}$ is volume of the solution (L), $\mathbf{c}_{0}$ represents the initial concentrations $(\mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}})$ of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ , $\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{e}}$ represents the equilibrium concentrations $(\mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}})$ of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ , and m is the dry mass of the adsorbent (g).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Construction of aerogel
The preparation of UNCAM is based the covalent cross-linking reaction between the amino groups in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and CS, which is different from the embedded and deposition processes to enhance the stability of MOF aerogel monolith. And there may also exist a self-crosslinking reaction of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CS in this synthesis process, respectively. The fabrication process of UNCAM was shown in Fig. 1. The aerogel monolith was synthesized through the sol-gel process with covalent cross-linking reaction followed by freeze-drying. Firstly, UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ bulk powders were uniformly dispersed in a chitosan solution to form a sol. Then, particles of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CS were used as the building blocks, and glutaraldehyde was used as the cross-linking agent to generate $\mathrm{C=N}$ using the covalent reaction and prepare UNCHM [47,48]. Finally, UNCAM with hierarchically porous structure was prepared by ice-water sublimation. The UNCAM has low density of $15.8~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and good shape stability, which can be positioned on the dandelion seeds without observable deformation as shown in Fig.1c. After the adsorption, the aerogel monolith can be directly extracted to recycle and the pure MOF particles are suspended in the waste-water as shown in Fig.1d.
The XRD curves of the related materials are shown in Fig. 2a. The UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ curve coincides with the results of reported literature [25] and simulated data. The adsorbed UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ with sharp peaks indicates that the crystal structure of the UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ remained intact. The lattice structure of UNCAM before and after adsorption was well maintained without significant loss of crystallinity observed, which indicates that cross-linking and adsorption do not change the structural stability of the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
The reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectra of CS and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ derivatives are shown in Fig. 2b, and all the relevant data is consistent with the literature reports [49, 50]. After crosslinking, a new peak is observed at $1651~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ on the spectra of UNCAM, which is the stretch vibration of $\mathrm{C=N}$ . The overlapping absorption peak at
$3422~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ becomes sharp, the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ absorption peak at $768~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ becomes smaller, and the peak at $1058\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the C-O stretching vibration of the saccharide structure. These observations confirmed that the materials are successful synthesized [48]. The reflectance FTIR spectrum of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ cross-linked product prepared by the reaction of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ with glutaraldehyde and without CS was also recorded for comparison. A new peak is observed at $1654\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . This is the stretch vibration of $\mathrm{C=N}$ which establishes that the amino group on UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can react with glutaraldehyde to form an imino group [48]. The reflectance FTIR spectrum of CS aerogel prepared by the reaction of CS with glutaraldehyde without UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was also recorded for comparison. A new peak is observed at $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . This is the stretch vibration of $\mathrm{C=N}$ 。 which establishes that the amino group on CS can react with glutaraldehyde to form an imino group [51, 52]. Therefore, we speculate that when UNCAM were constructed through the covalent cross-linking of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ and CS with glutaraldehyde as the crosslinker, a self-crosslinking reaction of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ and CS could also result.
Fig. 2c shows $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of the UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UNCAM before and after adsorption, respectively. The pore size distribution of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UNCAM before and after adsorption, respectively, is shown in Fig. S1. The pore size of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ demonstrated a marginal decrease from $1.47\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $1.41\ \mathrm{nm}$ before and after adsorption. The specific surface area of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ decreased from $912.784\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $882.876~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ before and after adsorption. The specific surface area and pore size of UNCAM are $514.082\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.41~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The high specific surface area of UNCAM could be attributed to MOF particle micropore, interpenetrating network structure and inter-particle pores. The surface area and pore size decreased to 427.802 $\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.23\mathrm{nm}$ , after the UNCAM’ adsorption of Pb(II), respectively. This change is consistent with the experimental effects of UNCAM’ cycles adsorption (All the
parameters are listed in Table.S1).
The TG analysis was carried out to calculate the mass residual ratio by the loading rate of CS, UNCAM and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and to derive the mass ratio of the two base materials. The mass residue values of $40.49\%$ and $19.11\%$ at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ correspond to the decomposition of CS and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , as shown in Fig. 2d. According to the reported method [41], the mass ratio of UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ and CS was calculated to be $50.83~\mathrm{wt\%}$ to $49.17~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , which is intensely close to the theoretical calculation values (50 wt% vs. $50\mathrm{wt\%}$ ). Therefore, the covalent cross-linking strategy proposed in this study is proved to be a highly effective method in preparing UNCAM with high MOF loadings.
From SEM images in Fig. 3a, there exist many micron-sized pores in the UNCAM that are connected by sublimation of ice crystals [41], and the structures of UNCAM can be clearly observed as entangled states. At higher magnifications (Fig. 3b, c, d and e), hierarchical macropores can be clearly observed. During the process of adsorption, the hierarchical macropores of UNCAM can adsorb considerable amounts of water and be conducive to the rapid flow of water [32]. The uniform distribution of particles on the surface and cross-section of the aerogel layer indicate that the MOF particles and polymer materials are interconnected to form an aerogel layer, as shown in Fig. 3e. Fig. 3f shows that the prepared MOF particles with sphere shape have good dispersibility, and the size of MOF particles are approximately $80\mathrm{nm}$ . The energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) spectra of UNCAM after adsorption are shown in Fig. S2. The Zr element appears evenly distributed in the aerogel, further indicating that the MOF particles are uniformly present in the aerogel. The uniform existence of a considerable amount of Pb element indicates the high capacity and favorable mass transfer of UNCAM.
## 3.3. Contrast adsorption experiments
Fig. 4. Adsorption of Pb(II) by CS, CS aerogel, UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ , and UNCAMs $(10\%,20\%$ , $30\%$ , $40\%$ , $50\%$ , wt, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\setminus}$ ), ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ concentration $=100\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ , adsorbent $\mathrm{dosage=0.4\:g{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ , and adsorption time $=360\mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$
CS aerogel prepared by the reaction of CS with glutaraldehyde without UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was also recorded for adsorption comparison. The adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ reached equilibrium adsorbed by CS, CS aerogel and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are $13.64~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ , $56.78~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ and $143.05~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ , respectively (Fig. 4). The $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ adsorption capacity values of CS and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}{\-}{\-}12$ are close to the respective reported values in the literature [53,54]. The porosity and specific surface area of the CS aerogel is increased and the dissolution is prevented, so the adsorption effect becomes better [55]. UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has a significantly higher adsorption capacity for Pb(II) than that of CS and CS aerogel. The adsorption capacity of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ increases proportionally with the increased MOF content in UNCAM, and this indicates that the MOF in UNCAM plays a leading role in the adsorption process. The quality of the adsorbents are the same in the static adsorption test, while the Pb(II) adsorption capacity of  UNCAM with $50\%$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ particles can reach $102.03\ \mathrm{mg{\cdotg-l}}$ $(1.612{\times}10^{6}\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-3})$ .  The flexibility of the overall material will be worse, if you continue to increase the mass ratio of MOF in UNCAM. The adsorption capacity of Pb(II) obtained in this work compared with the literature is similar with MOF powder and is larger than bio-char pellets (Table S2, supporting information). In the latest reported literature, the adsorption capacity of 1 $\mathrm{\JiO-}66{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}@\mathrm{CA}$ aerogel for $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ attain $89.40\ \mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ [39].The adsorption capacity in this work is $12.63~\mathrm{mg/g}$ more than UiO-66-NH2@CA aerogel, which is a high adsorption capacity to compared with literature reports (Table S2, supporting information).
## 3.4. Adsorption theory
## 3.4.1. Adsorption kinetics
The kinetic experiments were conducted using $5\mathrm{mg}$ adsorbent immersed in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of Pb(II) $(100~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{p H}=5$ , $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) as functions versus time. The results of this experiment are shown in Fig. 5a. Fast adsorption processes can be obtained in $60\mathrm{min}$ and 90 min for the UNCAM and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , respectively, because of the abundantly available adsorption sites in the initial stage [38]. Then the adsorption rate declines until it attains the adsorption equilibrium at $180\ \mathrm{min}$ and $300\ \mathrm{min}$ , respectively. The dynamics adsorption data were further studied by a pseudo-first- and pseudo-secondorder model using (Eq. S(1) and Eq. S(2), supporting information), respectively. Their fitting linear curves and calculated kinetic parameters are displayed in Figs. 5b and c and (Table S3, supporting information), respectively. By comparing the fitting curves and correlation coefficients $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ , it is observed that the pseudo-second-order model can describe the adsorption kinetics of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and UNCAM better than the pseudofirst-order model [39, 56]. The UNCAM’s adsorption rate is close to the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ particles.
## 3.4.2. Adsorption isotherms
Adsorption isotherms were applied to investigate the adsorption performances and mechanism of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and UNCAM, which are beneficial for understanding the effect of the interaction between the adsorbate and the adsorbent on the adsorption capacity. The concentration of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ was set to a series of $20{-}220~\mathrm{mg.L^{-1}}$ , and other conditions were unchanged. The amount of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ adsorbed always increased with an increase in the initial Pb(II) concentrations as shown in Fig. S3.. The thermodynamic adsorption data was analyzed using the (Eq. S(3) Langmuir, Eq. S(4) Freundlich, Eq. S(5) Temkin, Eq. S(5)-(8) Dubinin–Radushkevich, supporting information) .
The obtained isotherm parameters and linear fitting curves are shown in Tab.1 and (Fig. S3, supporting information), respectively. According to the fitted curve, $\mathrm{K_{L}}(>0)$ and $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ , the adsorption isotherms of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UNCAM are more consistent with the Langmuir model, indicating the adsorption process belongs to monolayer adsorption and chemisorption [57]. The n value from Freundlich model is greater than 1, which indicates it is a favorable adsorption [58]. The E value obtained from the Dubinin–Radushkevich model is greater than $16\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , indicating that it belongs to chemisorption [59].
## 3.4.3. Adsorption thermodynamics
The impact of temperature on $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ adsorption by UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and UNCAM was measured in the range of 293-313 K. In addition, the thermodynamics of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ adsorption were analyzed by the (Eq. S(9) and Eq. S(10), supporting information). The fitting curve and corresponding thermodynamic parameters are shown in Fig. 6 and (Table S4, supporting information), respectively.
(b) (Pb (II) concentration $=30\mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ , adsorbent dosage ${\bf\Phi}=0.4{\bf g}{\bf\cdot}{\bf L}^{-1}.$ ).
The adsorption of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and UNCAM is an endothermic process because the $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{0}$ value shows greater than 0. It is observed that $\Delta\mathrm{G}_{0}$ is negative at all temperatures, which indicates that the adsorption process is spontaneous and increases with increasing temperature [53]. The adsorption process of UNCAM follows the same rules as that of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ; however, the spontaneity is marginally lower [60].
## 3.4.4. Adsorption mechanisms
Pb (II) has no lone pair electrons, it can accept electrons from of O and N of basic groups [60]. And the adsorption of Pb (II) onto N can form a relatively stable coordination compound by chemical bonding [60]. The isoelectric point (IEP) of UiO$66\substack{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and UNCAM were determined to be 4.5 and 4.6, respectively (Fig. S5., supporting information). When $\mathsf{p H}{=}5.0{>}$ IEP, adsorbents were negatively charged. It is advantageous that the positively charged $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ ion is attracted and coordinated by the negatively charged adsorbents. Further investigation of the adsorbents before and after adsorption were conducted in order to elucidate the adsorptive mechanisms. The XPS spectra of UNCAM are shown in Fig. 7. Three main peaks of C1s, N1s, and O1s from the CS and the extra Zr3d peak of UNCAM are shown in Fig. 7a, which are similar to those in the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ framework. Pb4f peaks are observed after UNCAM adsorb Pb(II) [51,53]. Fig. 7b shows doublet peaks at $184.95\mathrm{eV}$ and $182.80\mathrm{eV}$ of $Z\mathrm{r}3\mathrm{d}$ from $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [53]. After the covalent reaction of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ with CS, the peak intensity of Zr3d in UNCAM appear significantly weakened, and Zr3d remains basically unchanged after UNCAM adsorb Pb(II). And this demonstrates the stability of the UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The N peaks in C-N of CS and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are appear in at 399.60 eV and $399.55\mathrm{eV}$ ，respectively (Figs. S4 a and b, supporting information). The C-N peak drops to lower energy at $399.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ after the covalent reaction, and a new peak $\mathrm{C=N}$ is observed at $400.00\mathrm{eV}$ in UNCAM, as shown in Fig. 7c [49], which verifies the characterization of the infrared spectrum and indicates the existence of a covalent crosslinking reaction. The C-N peak drops to lower energy at $398.90\mathrm{eV}$ after adsorption of Pb (II) by UNCAM, and a new peak $\mathrm{N^{+}}$ is observed at $401.70\mathrm{eV}$ , as shown in Fig. 7d. The adsorption mechanisms of coordination interaction between N and Pb (II) were confirmed. The peaks at 532.65 eV, 531.75 eV, and $532.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to O in C-O of CS, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , and UNCAM, respectively, as shown in Fig. 7e. After Pb(II) adsorb implementation, the binding energy of O1s in UNCAM increases by $0.65\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and then, the electron density of the oxygen atom diminishes because of the bonding with an electron-withdrawing neighbor atom. This phenomenon confirms the strong adsorption interaction between Pb(II) and O [5]. Moreover, it is observed from Fig. 7f that the Pb(II) adsorption of UNCAM is verified by the occurrence of a doublet (Pb 4f5/2 and 4f7/2), which indicates that the UNCAM can effectively capture the ions after the adsorption [53]. This also confirms the coordination interaction between amidogen and Pb (II)。
## 3.5. Reusability
To investigate the reusability of sorbent on UNCAM, the sorbent transferred into the $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ solution ( $\mathrm{100~mg/L)}$ at $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After adsorption, the adsorbent was regenerated with $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) solution and then washed and vacuum freeze-dried for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The same procedure was followed for other three adsorption-desorption measurements. The UNCAM retained $90.12\%$ adsorption ability after these 3 cycles as shown in Fig.8. In addition, the aerogels could be recycled easily, thereby avoiding secondary pollution.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we reported a novel method to prepare hierarchically porous MOFCS aerogel through covalent crosslinking with the UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ particles and CS as the building blocks. UNCAM possesses high surface areas, combined morphological, ultralow density and functional properties of both UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and CS. UNCAM have demonstrated outstanding performances in $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ adsorption treatment, comparable to the report literatures. In general, this production pathway can efficiently convert the MOF powders into a shapeable form and is promisingly applicable in more wastewater treatments.
